ttps://github.com/NVlabs/FoundationStereo.git)\nusing the PyTorch framework. On my ASUS device with an NVIDIA RTX 3050 GPU, I’m encountering a CUDA out of memory error when processing 1080p frames.\n\nWhen I resize the frames to 640p, the model runs successfully and generates a valid disparity map. But my requirement is to get disparity with 1080p.\nCould anyone suggest how to handle 1080p input frames without running into memory issues?\nI’d appreciate any recommendations related to model optimization, tiling, mixed precision, or memory management techniques.\n\nAdditionally, I’ve tested the NVIDIA TAO FoundationStereo model\n(https://catalog.ngc.nvidia.com/orgs/nvidia/teams/tao/models/foundationstereo?version=trainable_foundation_stereo_small_v1.0)\nexported to ONNX and deployed on a Jetson AGX device. However, I’m observing an inference time of around 2 minutes per frame when generating disparity maps, even though the benchmark mentioned in the Git repo indicates ~1 second inference on Jetson AGX.\n\nI have:\n\nCUDA version: 12.8\n\nONNX Runtime GPU version: 1.23.0\n\nIt seems the model is using CPU providers for execution instead of GPU, despite using the GPU-enabled ONNX Runtime package.\n\nCould someone please guide me on how to ensure GPU acceleration is used and reduce inference time to match the expected performance?\n\nThanks in advance for your help!--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nToolOrchestra/README.md at main · NVlabs/ToolOrchestra · GitHub (https://github.com/NVlabs/ToolOrchestra/blob/main/README.md)\nciteturn2279search8 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago;   * 2025/11/27: We release the code, training data, and model checkpoints of ToolOrchestra. ...     git clone https://github.com/NVlabs/ToolOrchestra.git\n\n  * 2025/12/2: 🏆 ToolOrchestra ranks #1 on GAIA benchmark!\n\n  * 2025/11/27: We release the code, training data, and model checkpoints of ToolOrchestra.\n\n* * *\n\n | \n\nWe introduce ToolOrchestra, a method for training small orchestrators that coordinate the use of intelligent tools. By using both tools and specialized models, ToolOrchestra surpasses GPT-5 while being much more efficient. Given a task, the Orchestrator alternates between reasoning and tool calling in multiple turns to solve it. The Orchestrator interacts with a diverse tool set, including basic tools (e.g., web search, code interpreter), specialized LLMs (e.g., coding models, math models), and generalist LLMs (e.g., GPT-5, Llama-Nemotron-Ultra-253B, Claude Opus 4.1). During training, Orchestrator is jointly optimized by outcome, efficiency, and preference rewards via end-to-end reinforcement learning. To aid RL training, we develop an automatic pipeline to synthesize both environment and tool-call tasks at scale.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBenchmark results for 11-33-40 and NVIDIA-TAO · Issue #180 · NVlabs/FoundationStereo · GitHub (https://github.com/NVlabs/FoundationStereo/issues/180)\nciteturn2279search9 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.1 years ago; Crawled: last month; NVlabs / FoundationStereo Public ... # Benchmark results for 11-33-40 and NVIDIA-TAO #180 ... opened on Sep 4, 2025\n\nNVlabs / FoundationStereo Public\n\n  * Notifications You must be signed in to change notification settings\n  * Fork 280\n  * Star\n\n  * Code\n  * Issues 78\n  * Pull requests 9\n  * Actions\n  * Security and quality 0\n  * Insights\n\n\n# Benchmark results for 11-33-40 and NVIDIA-TAO #180\n\nNew issue\n\nCopy link\n\nNew issue\n\nCopy link\n\nClosed\n\nClosed\n\nBenchmark results for 11-33-40 and NVIDIA-TAO#180\n\nCopy link\n\n## Description\n\nnijkah\n\nopened on Sep 4, 2025\n\nIssue body actions\n\nHi, thanks for your great work on this project.\n\nI’m interested in the benchmark results and performance of 11-33-40 and NVIDIA-TAO on the zero-shot generalization task (Table 2 in the paper).\nSince the evaluation code isn’t included here,